+
+# DrugFlow
+
+![DrugFlow](https://proto-bio.github.io/proto-assets/images/tool/drugflow/hero.png)
+
+> [!NOTE]
+> **License:** DrugFlow is open source and free for academic and commercial use under an MIT license. Please refer to [the license](https://github.com/LPDI-EPFL/DrugFlow/blob/main/LICENSE) for full terms. Trained checkpoints are published publicly on Zenodo and are auto-downloaded during setup, so no user training is required.
+
+## Overview
+
+[DrugFlow](https://github.com/LPDI-EPFL/DrugFlow) is a generative model for structure-based drug design that generates 3D small molecules inside a target protein pocket. Given a protein structure and a reference ligand that defines the pocket, the model proposes novel ligands with explicit 3D poses, each returned as canonical SMILES and an SDF mol block. This toolkit wraps the upstream model and samples pocket-conditioned molecules on GPU, returning one generation set per target protein, with each molecule carrying property metrics (QED, molecular weight, and heavy-atom count) alongside its generated pose.
+
+## Background
+
+DrugFlow ([Schneuing et al., 2025](https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.17815), ICLR 2025) integrates continuous flow matching with discrete Markov bridges to learn the chemical, geometric, and physical aspects of 3D protein-ligand data jointly. Flow matching transports a simple prior to the data distribution over continuous atomic coordinates, while discrete Markov bridges model categorical variables such as atom and bond types; together they let the model generate a complete molecule, geometry and chemistry at once, rather than placing atoms one at a time.
+
+Generation is pocket-conditioned: the model is given a protein structure and a reference ligand whose neighborhood defines the binding pocket, and it samples ligands that fit that pocket in three dimensions. Because the output is a posed molecule rather than only a SMILES string, each candidate arrives with a hypothesis for how it sits in the target, ready for downstream docking, scoring, or refinement. The upstream release also provides a confidence head that flags out-of-distribution samples, a preference-alignment variant that steers sampling toward more desirable metric values, and a FlexFlow variant that jointly samples protein side-chain angles to model receptor flexibility.
+
+### Learning Resources
+
+- [DrugFlow repository](https://github.com/LPDI-EPFL/DrugFlow) - source code, generation scripts, and checkpoint download links.
+- [DrugFlow paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.17815) (Schneuing et al., ICLR 2025) - the method, multi-domain distribution learning, and benchmark results.
+- [DrugFlow checkpoints on Zenodo](https://zenodo.org/records/14919171) - the public trained weights this toolkit downloads automatically.
+
+## Tools
+
+### DrugFlow Molecule Generation (`drugflow-generate`)
+
+Generates 3D small molecules conditioned on each target protein pocket, producing one generation set per protein. Each target is defined by a protein structure paired one-to-one with a reference ligand (an SDF block or `.sdf` path) whose neighborhood fixes the pocket; a single protein and reference are coerced into one-element lists. Every generated molecule is returned with its canonical SMILES, 3D pose SDF, and per-molecule property metrics. Import the entry points with `from proto_tools import run_drugflow_generate, DrugFlowGenerateInput, DrugFlowGenerateConfig`.
+
+#### Applications
+
+Use this tool to propose novel, pocket-aware ligands for a target of interest, to build a diverse candidate library for a known binding site, or to seed downstream docking, scoring, and filtering workflows with structures that already carry a plausible 3D binding pose. Because generation is conditioned on an explicit pocket, it is well suited to hit finding against a resolved or modeled receptor, to scaffold exploration around a known reference ligand, and to generating starting poses for molecular dynamics or free-energy workflows.
+
+#### Usage Tips
+
+- **A reference ligand is required to define the pocket.** Each protein must be paired with a reference ligand (an SDF block or `.sdf` path) carrying 3D coordinates; the pocket is the set of residues within `pocket_distance_cutoff` of that ligand. The `reference_ligands` list must align one-to-one with `proteins`, in the same order.
+- **Pick a `model_checkpoint` by bundled name.** The bundled checkpoints are `drugflow` (base model), `drugflow_ood` (adds a confidence head for out-of-distribution flagging), `flexflow` (also samples protein side-chain angles), and `drugflow_pa_comb` (preference-aligned). These are provisioned in the managed weights cache automatically; any other value is treated as a path to a custom `.ckpt`, which is not available on hosted workers, so run locally with `device="cuda"` when pointing at your own file.
+- **Raise `num_molecules_per_target` for larger libraries.** This is the number of molecules generated per protein pocket; molecules are sampled in `batch_size` chunks per GPU forward pass, so lower `batch_size` if a run exhausts GPU memory and raise it for throughput.
+- **Tune `pocket_distance_cutoff` to the pocket.** The default (8.0 Å) suits typical drug-like pockets; widen it for a larger or more open site and narrow it to focus on a tight subpocket. Leave `molecule_size` as `None` to sample sizes from DrugFlow's training-set distribution, or set it to cap heavy-atom count, and leave `n_steps` as `None` to use the checkpoint's default number of denoising steps.
+- **Use `seed` for reproducible batches.** When omitted, the framework draws a seed and advances it across every dispatch so repeated chunks still diverge; reuse the returned seed with the same checkpoint and configuration to reproduce a run.
+
+## Toolkit Notes
+
+<a href="https://bio-pro.mintlify.app/tools/guides/tool-persistence"><img src="https://img.shields.io/badge/Tool_Persistence_→-046e7a?style=flat-square&logo=readthedocs&logoColor=white" alt="Tool Persistence guide"></a> <a href="https://bio-pro.mintlify.app/tools/guides/device-management"><img src="https://img.shields.io/badge/Device_Management_→-046e7a?style=flat-square&logo=readthedocs&logoColor=white" alt="Device Management guide"></a> <a href="https://bio-pro.mintlify.app/tools/guides/parallel-execution"><img src="https://img.shields.io/badge/Parallel_Execution_→-046e7a?style=flat-square&logo=readthedocs&logoColor=white" alt="Parallel Execution guide"></a> <a href="https://bio-pro.mintlify.app/tools/guides/cloud-inference"><img src="https://img.shields.io/badge/Cloud_Inference_→-046e7a?style=flat-square&logo=readthedocs&logoColor=white" alt="Cloud Inference guide"></a>
+
+These apply to the DrugFlow tool in this toolkit (`drugflow-generate`).
+
+- **Runs on GPU.** Sampling the generative model uses a CUDA device; raise `batch_size` for throughput and lower it if the run exhausts GPU memory.
+- **Public checkpoints are downloaded automatically.** Unlike toolkits that require user-trained weights, DrugFlow's trained checkpoints are published publicly on [Zenodo](https://zenodo.org/records/14919171) and are fetched into the managed weights cache by `setup.sh` the first time the environment is built, so a bundled checkpoint name works out of the box.
+- **Generation is pocket-conditioned and produces posed ligands.** Each molecule is generated inside the pocket defined by its protein and reference ligand, and is returned with a 3D pose SDF in the pocket frame, not only a SMILES string.
+- **The tool is a CLI-subprocess wrapper over the upstream generator.** It builds an isolated standalone environment and drives the upstream `generate.py` as a subprocess, parsing its per-molecule output into the typed generation sets returned by this tool.
